---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedGlossaryLinks from '../../../components/RelatedGlossaryLinks.astro';

const layers = [
  {
    title: 'Marketplace page',
    copy: 'Collection pages, search pages, listings, and account screens may stop loading when a marketplace closes or changes product direction.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Token record',
    copy: 'A token record may remain readable on-chain if the chain, contract, and explorer or tool path remain available.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Metadata path',
    copy: 'Metadata may remain available, partially fail, or move depending on where it was stored and whether that storage path still resolves.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Media file',
    copy: 'Image, video, audio, or 3D media availability can differ from metadata availability. A marketplace closure alone does not settle it.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Wallet display',
    copy: 'A wallet or external viewer may still display an NFT if it can read the token and metadata path, even when the original marketplace no longer loads.'
  },
  {
    title: 'Trading path',
    copy: 'Trading may stop on the original marketplace while another marketplace, aggregator, or community tool may or may not support the asset.'
  }
];

const safeClaims = [
  'The original marketplace frontend appears unavailable.',
  'The collection page is no longer visible at the original URL.',
  'The marketplace operation ended according to the cited source.',
  'The asset path needs separate review.',
  'Wallet, metadata, and third-party marketplace visibility are not confirmed by this record.'
];

const unsafeClaims = [
  'The NFT disappeared.',
  'The asset was lost.',
  'The collection is gone forever.',
  'The marketplace was a scam.',
  'No one can access the asset anymore.'
];

const glossaryLinks = [
  { title: 'Asset metadata', href: '/glossary/asset-metadata/' },
  { title: 'Smart contract', href: '/glossary/smart-contract/' },
  { title: 'Collection page', href: '/glossary/collection-page/' },
  { title: 'Delisting', href: '/glossary/delisting/' }
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Do NFTs disappear when a marketplace closes? — Minted & Gone" description="A field-guide explanation of why marketplace closure does not automatically mean NFTs, metadata, media, wallets, or trading paths disappeared.">
  <div class="shell guide-page">
    <section class="field-hero simple-hero compact-hero">
      <div class="hero-botanical" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
      <div class="hero-copy plate-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
        <h1>Do NFTs disappear when a marketplace closes?</h1>
        <div class="flourish" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
        <p class="lede">Not automatically. A marketplace can disappear while other layers remain.</p>
        <p>This guide explains why a closed NFT marketplace frontend is not enough to claim that NFTs, metadata, media, wallets, or trading paths disappeared.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/encyclopedia/">Browse records</a><a class="btn" href="/guides/">Back to guides</a></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <p class="mock-note">This page is educational and archive-focused. It does not verify any individual token, ownership claim, recovery path, legal status, or asset value.</p>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Short answer</p>
      <h2>Marketplace closure is a frontend/product event, not a complete asset verdict</h2>
      <p>When a marketplace closes, the marketplace page, listings, search, account views, or trading interface may stop working. But token records, contracts, metadata, media, wallet display, third-party views, or archived pages may follow different paths.</p>
      <p>Minted &amp; Gone records those layers separately. A record should only claim disappearance when sources support the exact layer being described.</p>
    </section>

    <RelatedGlossaryLinks terms={glossaryLinks} />

    <section class="guide-grid numbered">
      {layers.map((layer, index) => (
        <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
          <span class="num">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <h2>{layer.title}</h2>
          <p>{layer.copy}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Safer wording</p>
      <h2>Say what the source actually supports</h2>
      <p>Most old marketplace records should use narrow, source-backed language. This keeps the archive useful without overstating loss or risk.</p>
      <div class="reading-guide-grid" aria-label="Safe and unsafe claim examples">
        <article><strong>Safer claims</strong><span>{safeClaims.join(' ')}</span></article>
        <article><strong>Avoid unless proven</strong><span>{unsafeClaims.join(' ')}</span></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">How M&amp;G records it</p>
      <h2>Records should separate what is gone from what remains</h2>
      <p>Marketplace pages should keep separate notes for what is gone, what remains, where users or assets may have gone, and which parts are unknown. A source may prove that a marketplace sunset happened while leaving the asset, metadata, and wallet questions unresolved.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn" href="/guides/frontend-vs-smart-contract-what-remains/">Frontend vs contract guide</a><a class="btn" href="/guides/how-to-check-old-nft-marketplace-pages/">Page checking guide</a><a class="btn" href="/methodology/">Methodology</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-grid">
      <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
        <h2>If the marketplace page is gone</h2>
        <p>Record the page state and archive URL. Do not automatically extend that claim to contracts, metadata, media, wallets, or other marketplaces.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
        <h2>If metadata is unclear</h2>
        <p>Use review flags. Unknown metadata state is better than a false claim that all assets disappeared or all assets remain safely accessible.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="framed-panel guide-card">
        <h2>If another market displays it</h2>
        <p>Record that as a continuity signal, not as proof that the original marketplace is still operating.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="framed-panel definition-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Related reading</p>
      <h2>Continue through the archive</h2>
      <p>Use this guide with individual records and evidence notes. The strongest record is often the one that admits which layer is still unknown.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/encyclopedia/">Browse marketplace records</a><a class="btn" href="/guides/what-happens-when-nft-marketplace-shuts-down/">Shutdown guide</a><a class="btn" href="/glossary/">Glossary</a></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>